<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Debal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Daybul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Debal was a port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, close to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, often identified with the excavated site of Banbhore. Active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a>, it was the first city taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> in his conquest of Sindh in 711–712, after attacks on Arab shipping nearby had given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> a pretext for the invasion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/debal.jpg" alt="Debal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shahid1024 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_Jinnah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
